<p>I will agree with you. Johnny doesn’t have a clear motivation for why he did this. But I’d argue that that’s just how some people are. Some people, when they see a boy playing with something he loves, will misinterpret their own unhappiness and feel such a hatred for this thing bringing joy for another person that they will feel compelled to destroy it. It’s common among children who are raised in broken homes and is a troupe in 80s movie bullies.</p>
<p>I don’t think at any point Johnny intended to kill Billy. Wells, despite what some people may think, are actually fairly easy to climb out of. However, you can’t do it with a bag in one hand. In Billy’s mind, as childish as it might seem, staying in that well with the kittens was better than trying to escape without them. He probably thought Johnny was gonna let him out, or somebody would be along to help him. But Johnny was gone, overcome by fear at his own actions, and nobody found him in that well for many years.</p>

